sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/darwin1859-ch05-12> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "A part developed to an extraordinary degree or in an extraordinary manner in one species, compared with the same part in closely allied species, tends to be highly variable within that species; the rule does not apply when the unusual part is shared by a whole group.";
    dc:type "empirical-generalization";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Parts unusually developed relative to congeners are highly variable";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Charles Darwin, On the Origin of Species (1st ed., London: John Murray, 1859), Chapter V, “Laws of Variation”; section “Parts developed in an unusual manner are highly variable: specific characters more variable than generic: secondary sexual characters variable.”. Scope: animals, especially birds, cirripedes and secondary sexual characters; Darwin could not confirm it for plants"
}
